WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.852 --> 00:00:04.961
Your project is done, and the sponsor wants to know: is it really better?

2
00:00:05.699 --> 00:00:10.308
Anyone who claims a result needs a baseline: the performance before the change,

3
00:00:10.482 --> 00:00:12.235
measured the same way as after.

4
00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:17.625
So the same definition, the same data source, the same period length,

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.228
and similar conditions.

6
00:00:20.392 --> 00:00:23.480
If the baseline counted stops over 5 minutes,

7
00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:28.089
and the after-data counts stops over 2, the comparison is worthless.

8
00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:33.651
Agree the definition at the start, write it down, and keep the raw data.

9
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.475
Then anyone can redo the sum.

10
00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:38.734
An example.

11
00:00:39.481 --> 00:00:43.602
At a bottling plant you measure downtime on line 3 for 12 weeks:

12
00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.354
the share of planned hours the line stands still.

13
00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.205
On average it is 14 percent.

14
00:00:50.238 --> 00:00:54.406
But every week is different: between 11 and 17 percent.

15
00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:59.234
That is noise: the normal week-to-week swing that has nothing to do with you.

16
00:01:00.224 --> 00:01:05.495
If downtime drops 2 points after four weeks, that still falls inside the band.

17
00:01:06.291 --> 00:01:07.278
It proves little.

18
00:01:08.287 --> 00:01:11.236
So measure roughly as many weeks after as before.

19
00:01:11.986 --> 00:01:14.714
A common rule of thumb is 8 to 12 weeks.

20
00:01:16.026 --> 00:01:20.636
Also check what else changed: volume, product mix, season, new staff.

21
00:01:21.618 --> 00:01:24.382
The strongest evidence is a comparison group:

22
00:01:24.602 --> 00:01:27.969
a similar line or region that did not get the change.

23
00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:30.124
Here that is line 4.

24
00:01:31.135 --> 00:01:33.654
After the changes you measure for 10 weeks.

25
00:01:34.422 --> 00:01:38.880
Line 3 is now at 9 percent, with weeks between 7 and 11.

26
00:01:39.604 --> 00:01:43.482
Line 4, where nothing changed, stays at 13 percent.

27
00:01:44.522 --> 00:01:48.760
The two bands barely overlap, and the comparison line did not move.

28
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.633
So the effect is very likely real.

29
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:54.624
Experts argue about this.

30
00:01:55.311 --> 00:01:57.563
Some want a formal statistical test.

31
00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:03.203
Most practitioners settle for a run chart, weekly values plotted over time,

32
00:02:03.319 --> 00:02:04.793
plus a comparison group.

33
00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:07.489
Watch for regression to the mean.

34
00:02:08.168 --> 00:02:10.780
Projects often start after a bad spell,

35
00:02:10.978 --> 00:02:14.043
and then the numbers drift back toward normal anyway.

36
00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:17.101
Part of your gain may be just that.

37
00:02:18.165 --> 00:02:21.950
And a result that fades once you leave is another warning sign.

38
00:02:22.661 --> 00:02:25.204
Measure again three months after closing.

39
00:02:26.582 --> 00:02:28.905
Then the money, in the client's terms.

40
00:02:29.637 --> 00:02:34.293
Freed hours are only money if something changes on the books: overtime cut,

41
00:02:34.444 --> 00:02:38.101
a shift dropped, more product sold, or an investment avoided.

42
00:02:39.162 --> 00:02:42.250
At a grid operator it may be customers connected sooner,

43
00:02:42.401 --> 00:02:47.394
or fewer customer minutes lost: minutes without power, added up over all customers.

44
00:02:48.421 --> 00:02:50.441
If extra output can be sold,

45
00:02:50.592 --> 00:02:55.491
value it at contribution margin: the sales price minus variable costs,

46
00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:57.558
such as material and energy.

47
00:02:58.527 --> 00:03:00.443
And let the controller check the sum:

48
00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:03.485
the finance person who owns the client's numbers.

49
00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:06.258
Back to the bottling plant.

50
00:03:07.038 --> 00:03:10.300
Line 3 is planned for 6,000 hours a year.

51
00:03:10.993 --> 00:03:17.460
From 14 to 9 percent is 5 percentage points less downtime: 300 extra running hours.

52
00:03:18.446 --> 00:03:22.208
The plant is sold out, so those hours become extra product.

53
00:03:23.251 --> 00:03:28.220
The controller values an hour at 450 euro of contribution margin.

54
00:03:28.912 --> 00:03:36.006
That is 300 hours times 450 euro: about 135,000 euro a year.

55
00:03:37.018 --> 00:03:39.933
You present it together with the controller, who checked it.

56
00:03:41.357 --> 00:03:43.691
Then the people side: don't overclaim.

57
00:03:44.404 --> 00:03:46.447
Say what you know, and what you don't.

58
00:03:47.475 --> 00:03:50.784
For example: downtime went from 12 to 8 percent.

59
00:03:51.555 --> 00:03:54.481
About one point is likely the lower summer volume.

60
00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:57.469
The rest we attribute to the changes.

61
00:03:58.511 --> 00:04:02.784
Welcome a sceptical controller: their approval is worth more than your slide.

62
00:04:03.545 --> 00:04:06.146
And give the credit to the team that did the work.

63
00:04:07.204 --> 00:04:11.558
Ask yourself one sharp question: if a board member challenges this number,

64
00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.427
who besides me will defend it?
